1  ZK
   The Halls Of ZK is an sophisticated adventure game which takes
   place at Digital's Spitbrook software engineering facility.
   Meet famous Spitbrook personalities!  Incredible treasures and
   mind-boggling encounters await you.  Exercise your computer-
   related skills to solve the myriad of obstacles that lead to
   the acquisition of the ultimate treasure!

   Format

     ZK

2  Credits

   The Halls of ZK was written by William Lees and Edmund Sullivan
   sometime in August 1985 for the DEC family open day at ZKO.
   Since that time a VAX binary and a VEST'd Alpha binary (with
   some source) has been floating around the 'net at various
   websites.

   Then, in about 2013 the full source code, including the source
   for the Interactive Fiction Compiler (IFC) was located by John
   Reagan.  To make the IFC portable the code generator was
   re-written to output MACRO-32 source modules by Tim Sneddon and
   as such there are now native versions for all OpenVMS supported
   architectures available.

   Thanks also go to Joe Ferraro for starting the quest to find
   and port the game from OpenVMS VAX to I64.

   Then, in 2021 The Halls of ZK became the first computer game to
   be built and played on the newly ported OpenVMS x86-64, beating
   the original ADVENT text adventure significantly due to the
   early delivery of the Pascal compiler.

   Thanks too to Jake Hamby, who fixed saving games and random
   numbers on Alpha, I64 and x86-64, and to Brian Chiha, who
   delivered an excellent walkthrough.

   The Halls of ZK is now an open source project maintained by Tim
   Sneddon with the grateful support of Endless Software Solutions.

2  Support

   There is no formal support for The Halls Of ZK, it is provided
   "as is".  The product website can be found here:

      https://hallsofzk.org

   At this website there is documentation, downloads and a
   community mailing list, please feel free to join.
